 ## Project
 
-Fledge (the `fledge` gem, https://github.com/setdef/Fledge) ships a Ruby app to people who don't have Ruby. It copies an app, the program its developer already runs (`exe/my_cool_app`, `bin/server`, or a `main.rb`) with its project and the gems its Gemfile.lock locks, into the runtime for the platform it runs on: a relocatable CRuby plus a small C launcher that embeds it and runs the program, with Bundler set up as `bundle exec` would (ADR 005). Then it archives the result. Runtimes are prebuilt per platform by Fledge's own CI (ADR 001), and each platform's app is built on a computer of that platform, with that platform's own tools (ADR 010). Targets use RubyGems platform names: `arm64-darwin`, `x86_64-linux`, `aarch64-linux`, `x86_64-linux-musl` and `aarch64-linux-musl` (whose Ruby Fledge's CI builds from source in Alpine; ADR 017), and `x64-mingw-ucrt`. App kinds are `console` and `windowed`: a windowed app is an `.app` bundle on macOS, signed ad hoc, a GUI program on Windows, and a folder with a desktop entry on Linux, and every app carries the name, icon, version, and identifier its `fledge.yml` gives, where each system shows them (ADR 008); a windowed app also gets its platform's installer, a `.dmg`, an NSIS setup program, or an AppImage (ADR 009). Mobile runtimes for iOS and iPadOS and for Android (`arm64-ios`, `arm64-ios-simulator`, `aarch64-linux-android`, `x86_64-linux-android`) are CRuby built from source as an XCFramework and an AAR with a small embedding API (ADR 011); `fledge build ios` (on a Mac) and `fledge build android` (on a Mac, x86-64 Linux, or Windows) write an Xcode or Gradle project around them for a pure-Ruby app, which the developer builds, signs, and submits (ADR 012). Framework wrappers (Lota for 2D games, Tokra for desktop apps, Rooibos for terminal UIs) will call Fledge. The plans of record are `doc/contributors/plan/001.md` (the walking skeleton) and `doc/contributors/plan/002.md` (toward 1.0); what the maintainer has decided is in `doc/contributors/decisions.md`.
